E
book standard god~
This book explains the rules governing standard god eq.

*These objects were created to be sure all god items were unusable by
 mortals, thus &=LRDO NOT&N make any of your god items non-transient
 or not opposed to all alignments. Having the items this way keeps mortals
 from ever abusing them in any fashion.

*You should not have any god items until you can be responsible for the
 commands to string and load the items. Otherwise, you distract other gods
 who may be trying to do serious work.

*Having these objects instead of separate god objects lessens the space
 required in the object files, freeing up space for more standard objects
 with better purposes. Also, these items can be changed whenever you like
 via string and setbit as opposed to a higher level god having to redo the
 object files.

*Gods should ONLY use these items, just in case they should be sac'd, drop
 items or any such mishaps. These items are specifically made unusable by
 mortals where as other loaded items are not.

Also see TIPS
~
E
tips~
These are tips for use of the standard god objects.

1.) If you use tintin or another client, set up aliases or macros to string
    your objects to save time. Dropping your items will and SHOULD destroy
    them.

2.) Object 1211 is the book of Duris ansi codes which describes how to use
    colors in your objects.

3.) Conform your objects to those you want to express your god's ideals.


4.) The standard god objects are vnums #1280 - #1299, insignia are secret (un-
    seeable by mortals) and allow detect good and evil as many gods like that.

Stringing commands:

&+Lstring o <keyword> name <new keyword>&N
-->This changes the objects keyword to the new keyword.

&+Bstring o <keyword> short <short description>&N
-->What you see with equipment or inventory commands, dropping, or giving
   the item.

&+Gstring o <keyword> long <long description>&N
-->What you see when the object is on the ground.

&+Ystring o <keyword> desc <keyword for extra description>&N
-->Strings field for an object description when you type look object.
~
