different pages by 'look page1' 'look page2' and so on.
~
E
page1~
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
:                                                                      :
:  ... long ages past Amberyl was the greatest bard that ever walked   :
:  the land.  Her voice did not _make_ music -- it _was_ music.  Her   :
:  Deeds were known far and wide ... but yet she was not happy.  The   :
:  Greatest source of her sorrow was that she had no instrument to     :
:  match her matchless voice ... so her lifelong quest was to find a   :
:  maker who could possibly fashion a lute so pure and good that it    :
:  would never break, and one of sound tone and tune that would        :
:  satisfy her.                                                        :
:                                                                      :
:  It is said that after traveling far and wide (even farther and      :
:  wider than most bards go) she found a wizard.  The wizard agreed to :
:  make the instrument for her, aided by his arcane arts.  For, of     :
:  course, a price.  Amberyl, without a moment's hesitation, agreed    :
:  and departed for a year without even asking the price.              :
:                                                                      :
:  The mage withdrew for the year to create the lyre, and during that  :
:  year Amberyl roamed the land, longing for the instrument so deeply  :
:  that all known compositions by her that year are melancholy or      :
:  filled with sadness.                                                :
:                                                                      :
~
E
page2~
:  At last the appointed day came.  Amberyl found the mage, who held   :
:  forth the fruits of his labor.                                      :
:                                                                      :
:  It was the finest lute ever crafted.  Amberyl brushed her fingers   :
:  across the strings and the sound that came forth was purer than     :
:  ever was heard in the mortal realm -- then or since.                :
:                                                                      :
:  Amberyl, with eyes aglow, asked what the price was; she was willing :
:  to pay it on the spot.                                              :
:                                                                      :
:  The mage smiled sadly.  'What price can one pay to allow such music :
:  in the realm of men?' he asked.  'It does not belong here.  It is   :
:  another world entirely.  One cannot possess such music and remain   :
:  here for long, so close to mortal sorrow and woe.  There is no      :
:  price for this.'  The mage held up a hand before Amberyl could      :
:  reply.  'There is, instead, a limit.  Every time you play the lyre  :
:  You will age ... the more you play the lyre the faster you will age :
:  until the winds of time claim you for their own.  The lute will not :
:  age the player after its first owner has succumed to time's ravages :
:  but the first owner must pay this price.'                           :
~
E
page3~
:                                                                      :
:  Amberyl went away saddened.  And eventually, unable to bear being   :
:  without the music, she played ... and played ... and played.        :
:                                                                      :
:  She has passed to the next world, but her wonderous lute is still   :
:  in this world.  Perhaps longing for the music ties her to this      :
:  plane yet.  For, after all, the lute's price has been paid.         :
:                                                                      :
:----------------------------------------------------------------------:
~
