page1 1~

                         &+RDon't Be a Fool, Close the Book.
                         &+R   Its Full of Nothing But
                         &+R            Lies!

   In the beginning there was nothing.  Just vast empty space, rolling away
into the darkness, yet never getting anywhere.  Finally, nothing got bored and
created something.  That something was, is, Duris.  But nothing was no good at
playing god, and made many mistakes when trying to make the mud stand.  Some
mud was evil, some mud was good, but all in all the mud was confused.  One day
good mud fought evil mud, and they both split into many different muds.  Duris
became overcrowded.  This is the story of one good mud named Kaynor.

   Kaynor was a mud of integrity, he was one of the first knights to use the
name Paladin.  This paladin mud walked far and near, fought many evil mud, and
amassed much shiny metal wealth.  He fell in love with a mud woman who called
herself Tharnadia.  They married, and went off to found a mudslide.  However,
the woman mud became sick, she began to dry out until finally she was just
dirt (and dirt unlike mud can't talk).  Kaynor became upset and spent all his
shiny metal building a city in honor of her.  This city was small for although
he had lots of shiny metal, it didn't go that far back then.  In time this
mud, too, became dirt that doesn't speak.  And they buried him incase he
decided he could speak (people fear talking dirt).  In time the city grew, and
they built a stronghold to lock away their prisoners.  This they called Caer
Tannud.  As more and more poeple came, a second keep was errected to house the
government.  This keep they named Caer Dauthe.  And so it was for four hundred
years, until Duris got angry.  He shook with all his might to be rid of the
mud that talks.  Many mud did indeed turn to dirt, but some mud survived.

   The city and its keeps, did too survive, although now the keeps were five
thousand feet above the city.  One of the keeps moved to an island mesa,
prefering the economically stable waterfront property, while the city decided
to drop twenty feet below its roads.  But the walking mud did not care, they
moved right back into the buildings, and built new roads.  And so it has been
for nearly three thousand years.  And walking mud still turns to static dirt,
except sometimes static dirt does indeed decide to talk.

                                   &+LKabanon&n
~
